<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Cutch State</h1><p class="st-meta">1147 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Bhuj</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Jadeja Rajput kingdom of Kutch, a semi-island of salt marsh and desert between Sindh and Gujarat, with its own sea trade, coinage and court arts.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadeja">Jadeja Rajputs</a> came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and settled in Kutch in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>; the register's date reflects tradition rather than firm record. Several Jadeja branches competed until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khengarji-i">Rao Khengarji</a> I united the country in the mid-16th century and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuj">Bhuj</a> his capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kutch was cut off by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rann-of-kutch">Rann</a> but faced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>, and its sailors and merchants traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandvi-kutch">Mandvi</a>. The state issued its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> kori coins. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakhpatji">Rao Lakhpatji</a> (r. 1741–1761) patronised poets and craftsmen, and his reign produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aina-mahal">Aina Mahal</a> palace. The Rao ruled with powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadeja">Jadeja</a> kinsmen holding estates.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Treaties with the British from 1816 made Kutch a protected state, and in 1819 a great earthquake struck. Kutch acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1947 and in 1948 became a centrally governed province, later joining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a> and, in 1960, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cutch-state.jpg" alt="Cutch State" width="320" height="161"><figcaption>Image: Original: Dr. Andreas Birken Vector: Lommes, 燃灯 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cutch_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1147 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bhuj</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Khengarji I, Lakhpatji, Khengarji III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kutchi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kutch, Kachchh</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1548&amp;focus=cutch-state" aria-label="Cutch State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6540 1320 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cutch State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6860 1576L7018 1385M6860 1576L7364 1601M6860 1576L7364 1602M6860 1576L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1385h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6860 1576h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6860 1576h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bhuj. <a href="/atlas?year=1548&amp;focus=cutch-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
